    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Sinorhizobium meliloti, an agriculturally important α-proteobacterium, invades the roots of certain plants to form a nitrogen-fixing symbiotic relationship. During this process, both partners exchange signals and co-differentiate to form a root nodule. At several stages in root nodule formation, S. meliloti transcribes genes that are associated with cell envelope stress. However, the pathways by which S. meliloti responds to cell envelope stress are not well understood and appear to be distinct from the pathways in better-studied bacteria such as E. coli. Previous research probing the S. meliloti response to cell envelope stress has shown that a novel operon esrRAB is strongly upregulated in response to deoxycholate, a generic membrane stressor. However, no research so far has uncovered the pathway responsible for transducing this signal. This work used a mutagenesis screen to identify proteins required for the esr transcriptional response to deoxycholate. Through this screen, seven genes were found to be required for the activation of esr including two transcription factors, emrR and irr. Homologs of another gene revealed in the screen, ppx2, have been found to be capable of cleaving guanosine pentaphosphate into the “alarmone” guanosine tetraphosphate. Further elucidating the pathways used for detecting and responding to cell envelope stress may provide important insights into signaling pathways in other α-proteobacteria. Furthermore, this research may eventually allow more efficient inoculation techniques in commercial alfalfa agriculture through a more comprehensive understanding of the response of S. meliloti to various stresses.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
